    "4": [
        {
            "presentation_step_number": "1",
            "title": "Continuous Loop",
            "narration_statement": "These four components work together in a continuous loop. The agent perceives, reasons, acts, then perceives again based on the result of that action.",
            "matching_section": "Purpose: Explain Continuous Loop in Agent-Environment Interaction Loop\nDominant Visual: Animated circular flow diagram of Perception-Reasoning-Action.\nVisual Choreography: The four component modules (Perception, Memory, Reasoning, Action) from the previous scene re-arrange into a clean circular flow. Animated, pulsing arrows connect them: Perception -> Reasoning -> Action -> (back to) Perception. Memory sits centrally, connected to Reasoning. A central label 'Agent Loop' appears. The 'Environment' ring pulses, indicating continuous input/output.\nPersistence: Clean anchor across steps."
        },
        {
            "presentation_step_number": "2",
            "title": "Agent vs. Static Tool",
            "narration_statement": "That loop is what we call agent environment interaction. And it's worth understanding because it's what separates an agent from a static tool. A static tool waits to be asked. An agent operates within an environment. It observes what's happening, decides what action is appropriate, and executes. Then it checks the result, and decides what comes next.",
            "matching_section": "Purpose: Explain Agent vs. Static Tool in Agent-Environment Interaction Loop\nDominant Visual: Side-by-side comparison of a passive static tool and an active, looping agent.\nVisual Choreography: The 'Agent Loop' shrinks slightly and moves to the right (x=2.5). A new visual appears on the left (x=-2.5): a dormant, gray 'Static Tool' icon (e.g., a wrench or a simple calculator) with a 'waiting' animation (e.g., an idle blinking cursor), labeled 'Static Tool'. An arrow points from a 'User Input' icon towards the Static Tool, which then emits a 'Result' (fades out). The Agent Loop on the right, labeled 'AI Agent', continues its active P-R-A cycle, with arrows flowing to and from the 'Environment' ring. A comparison label 'Agent vs. Tool' appears at the top center. The 'Agent Environment Interaction' label appears near the Agent Loop.\nPersistence: Clean anchor across steps."
        }
    ],
    "5": [
        {
            "presentation_step_number": "1",
            "title": "Monitoring Grid Performance",
            "narration_statement": "At Solara, an agent monitoring grid performance isn't just waiting for someone to run a report. It's watching. When output at a site drops below threshold, it observes the deviation, decides the appropriate response, which might be querying historical data, cross-referencing weather records, or escalating to a human operator, and takes that action.",
            "matching_section": "Purpose: Explain Monitoring Grid Performance in Dynamic Interaction Example\nDominant Visual: Solara agent actively observing and reacting to a critical event.\nVisual Choreography: A simplified 'Solara Site' icon (e.g., a solar panel array) is at the center-left. A 'Performance Gauge' (e.g., a dial with a needle) connected to the site shows output. The needle drops below a [Threshold] line, triggering a red pulse. A 'Monitoring Agent' icon (smaller version of our AI Core) appears, sending a 'Perception' signal to itself. From the agent, a 'Decision Tree' sprouts, highlighting branches for [Query Data], [Cross-Ref Weather], [Escalate Human]. The 'Escalate Human' branch glows red, and an arrow points to a 'Human Operator' icon. The agent then performs the chosen 'Action'.\nPersistence: Clean anchor across steps."
        },
        {
            "presentation_step_number": "2",
            "title": "Bi-Directional Relationship",
            "narration_statement": "The environment shapes the agent. The agent shapes the environment. That bi-directional relationship is what makes agents useful in dynamic real-world data contexts. Let's slow that loop down and look at each stage.",
            "matching_section": "Purpose: Explain Bi-Directional Relationship in Dynamic Interaction Example\nDominant Visual: Animated arrows showing reciprocal influence between agent and environment.\nVisual Choreography: The Monitoring Agent and Solara Site persist. A large, circular, animated arrow emerges, flowing from 'Environment' (represented by the Solara Site and its data) towards the 'Agent'. Simultaneously, another large, circular animated arrow flows from the 'Agent' back towards the 'Environment'. These arrows pulse in sync, labeled 'Shapes Agent' and 'Shapes Environment'. A central label 'Bi-Directional' appears. The P-R-A loop from the previous scene subtly re-emerges around the agent, ready to be 'slowed down'.\nPersistence: Clean anchor across steps."
        }
    ],
    "6": [
        {
            "presentation_step_number": "1",
            "title": "Perception: Input Intake",
            "narration_statement": "Perception is input. The agent receives information, a data reading, a user request, a tool result, and error message. It takes that in and adds it to its working context.",
            "matching_section": "Purpose: Explain Perception: Input Intake in Perception-Reasoning-Action Cycle Deep Dive\nDominant Visual: Inflowing data streams filling the Perception module and populating context.\nVisual Choreography: Top-right PIP active. Main canvas: A large, central 'Perception Module' icon (e.g., an eye or sensor) glows. Multiple animated data streams flow into it, labeled [Data Reading], [User Request], [Tool Result], [Error Message]. As they enter, a 'Working Context' panel (e.g., a stack of cards) adjacent to the Perception Module fills up with these inputs. The Perception Module pulses as it processes input.\nPersistence: Clean anchor across steps."
        },
        {
            "presentation_step_number": "2",
            "title": "Reasoning: Interpretation & Plan",
            "narration_statement": "Reasoning is interpretation and planning. The agent's LLM process is what it's received, considers its goal, reviews what it's already done, and decides what action to take next. This is where the intelligence lives.",
            "matching_section": "Purpose: Explain Reasoning: Interpretation & Plan in Perception-Reasoning-Action Cycle Deep Dive\nDominant Visual: LLM core processing inputs, evaluating goal, and formulating a plan.\nVisual Choreography: The 'Perception Module' shrinks and moves to the left. A large, central 'Reasoning Engine' (LLM icon, e.g., a stylized brain with neural pathways) appears and glows. Arrows flow from the 'Working Context' (from previous step, now smaller) into the Reasoning Engine. Inside, small icons representing [Goal], [Past Actions] appear and are referenced. The LLM pulses with internal thought bubbles and connections, culminating in a 'Plan' output (e.g., a small flowchart icon).\nPersistence: Clean anchor across steps."
        },
        {
            "presentation_step_number": "3",
            "title": "Action: Execution & Cycle Restart",
            "narration_statement": "Action is execution. The agent calls a tool, writes an output, sends a message, or updates a record. Something changes in the world as a result. Then the cycle begins again. The result of the action becomes new perception. The agent reasons about what that result means. For Solaira's Equipment Help Monitoring Agent, this cycle might run dozens of times in a single task. Quarrying data, evaluating results, deciding what to check next, generating a finding, before it produces a final output.",
            "matching_section": "Purpose: Explain Action: Execution & Cycle Restart in Perception-Reasoning-Action Cycle Deep Dive\nDominant Visual: Execution of actions, showing impact on environment and feedback loop.\nVisual Choreography: The 'Reasoning Engine' shrinks and moves to the left. A large, central 'Action Layer' (robotic arm/network icon) appears. The 'Plan' from Reasoning flows into Action. From Action, outward-flowing animated signals appear: [Call Tool], [Write Output], [Send Message], [Update Record]. These signals impact a subtle, dynamic 'Environment' background. A circular arrow then animates from the 'Environment' back to where 'Perception' would be, labeled 'New Perception'. The entire P-R-A loop (Perception-Reasoning-Action) then quickly cycles multiple times, illustrating the 'dozens of times' for the Solara agent, with small labels like [Querying Data], [Evaluating Results], [Generating Finding] appearing and disappearing rapidly during the cycles. Finally, a single 'Final Output' document icon emerges.\nPersistence: Clean anchor across steps."
        }
    ],
    "7": [
        {
            "presentation_step_number": "1",
            "title": "Types of Agents",
            "narration_statement": "Now let's talk about the three main types of agents, because not every problem needs the same architecture.",
            "matching_section": "Purpose: Explain Types of Agents in Reactive & Goal-Based Agents\nDominant Visual: Introduction of agent types with a branching visual.\nVisual Choreography: A central 'Agent Types' header appears. Below it, three empty, labeled slots or sections appear horizontally, ready for 'Reactive', 'Goal-Based', and 'Utility-Based' agents. The 'Reactive' slot highlights.\nPersistence: Clean anchor across steps."
        },
        {
            "presentation_step_number": "2",
            "title": "Reactive Agent: Rule-Driven",
            "narration_statement": "The first type is the reactive agent. A reactive agent responds to its current input using predefined rules. It has no memory of what happened before. No ability to plan ahead. It simply observes the condition and responds. Think of a simple alert agent as Solara. If output at Site 12 drops below 8% of a baseline, send a notification. That's a reactive agent. Fast, predictable, easy to build, but limited. It can't investigate why the drop happened, or determine whether the alert is actually worth sending.",
            "matching_section": "Purpose: Explain Reactive Agent: Rule-Driven in Reactive & Goal-Based Agents\nDominant Visual: A simple condition-action switch mechanism.\nVisual Choreography: Inside the 'Reactive' slot, a visual mechanism appears: A 'Sensor' icon (e.g., a waveform) detects an 'Input Condition' ([Output < 8%]). This triggers a direct, fast-pulsing arrow to a 'Rule Engine' (e.g., a gear icon), which instantly activates an 'Action' (e.g., a notification bell icon labeled [Send Alert]). A small 'No Memory' icon and 'No Plan' icon float above it. The entire mechanism glows green for 'Fast' and 'Predictable', then briefly flashes red with 'Limited' text. The Solara Site icon from earlier could briefly appear with a dropping gauge and an alert.\nPersistence: Clean anchor across steps."
        },
        {
            "presentation_step_number": "3",
            "title": "Goal-Based Agent: Planned Outcomes",
            "narration_statement": "The second type is the goal-based agent. A goal-based agent reasons about actions in terms of whether they move it closer to a defined outcome. It plans and evaluates options. It chooses the path most likely to achieve the goal. Solara's Site Performance Investigation Agent works this way. Its goal is to determine why Site 47 under-performed last Tuesday. It doesn't follow a fixed script. It decides what data to pull based on what it finds, adjusting its approach as results come in.",
            "matching_section": "Purpose: Explain Goal-Based Agent: Planned Outcomes in Reactive & Goal-Based Agents\nDominant Visual: Agent navigating a branching path towards a defined goal, evaluating options.\nVisual Choreography: Inside the 'Goal-Based' slot, a visual mechanism appears: A 'Goal Node' (e.g., a target icon labeled [Under-performance Cause]) is at the right. An 'Agent' icon (our AI Core) is at the left. From the agent, multiple branching paths (decision points) emerge. The agent icon navigates these paths, pausing at each branch point to 'Evaluate Options' (e.g., small question marks appear). A 'Path Score' (e.g., a progress bar) increases as it moves closer to the goal. The chosen path illuminates, showing dynamic adjustment. Small data icons (e.g., [Pull Data]) appear along the chosen path. The 'Fixed Script' label appears with a red 'X' over it.\nPersistence: Clean anchor across steps."
        }
    ],
    "8": [
        {
            "presentation_step_number": "1",
            "title": "Utility-Based Agent: Optimized Trade-offs",
            "narration_statement": "The third type is the utility-based agent. A utility-based agent goes further than goal-based reasoning. It doesn't just ask, will this action achieve the goal? It asks, which available action achieves the goal most efficiently, at the lowest cost, and with the highest confidence. At Solara, a utility-based agent managing report generation across hundreds of sites needs to make trade-off decisions constantly. Do I generate a full diagnostic report or a summary? Do I escalate the anomaly or log it for review? It evaluates options against a utility function and picks the best one.",
            "matching_section": "Purpose: Explain Utility-Based Agent: Optimized Trade-offs in Utility-Based Agent & Complete Workflow\nDominant Visual: Decision matrix with weighted trade-offs and optimization.\nVisual Choreography: Inside the 'Utility-Based' slot from the previous scene, a visual mechanism appears: A 'Decision Matrix' (e.g., a 2x2 grid) emerges, with rows representing 'Actions' (e.g., [Full Report], [Summary], [Escalate], [Log]) and columns representing 'Metrics' ([Efficiency], [Cost], [Confidence]). Each cell contains a numerical 'Utility Score'. An 'Agent' icon (our AI Core) hovers, dynamically highlighting the highest-scoring action path after evaluating all options. A 'Utility Function' graph (e.g., a small curve) appears briefly to indicate the optimization process. The chosen action path glows.\nPersistence: Clean anchor across steps."
        },
        {
            "presentation_step_number": "2",
            "title": "Complete Agent Workflow",
            "narration_statement": "Let's zoom out and look at how these components and concepts come together in a complete agent workflow. A goal arrives The agent perceives it, adds it to context, and begins reasoning. It identifies the first action needed and executes. The results come back. The agent evaluates whether it's closer to the goal, it plans the next action, it executes again. This continues, perceive, reason, act, until the goal is reached. An error condition is triggered, or the agent determines it cannot proceed and hands off to a human.",
            "matching_section": "Purpose: Explain Complete Agent Workflow in Utility-Based Agent & Complete Workflow\nDominant Visual: Full P-R-A loop with goal progression and potential error conditions.\nVisual Choreography: A comprehensive 'Agent Workflow' diagram appears center-stage. It's a larger version of the P-R-A loop from earlier, but with added elements: A 'Goal' input node starts the flow. Arrows lead from 'Goal' to 'Perceive', then 'Reason', then 'Act', then back to 'Perceive'. A 'Goal Progress' bar updates with each cycle. An 'Error Condition' node with a red lightning bolt icon branches off the main loop. The entire loop animates, showing data packets flowing and nodes illuminating sequentially. When the 'Goal' is met, a 'Goal Achieved' badge appears. Or, if 'Error Condition' is triggered, the flow diverts.\nPersistence: Clean anchor across steps."
        },
        {
            "presentation_step_number": "3",
            "title": "Responsible Handoff",
            "narration_statement": "What's important to notice is that workflow is the handoff point. Good agent design always includes a point at which the agent recognizes the limits of its confidence and escalates. That's not a failure, it's responsible architecture. At Solara, an agent that flags an equipment anomaly, it can't fully explain and routes it to a human engineer isn't falling short. It's doing exactly what it should.",
            "matching_section": "Purpose: Explain Responsible Handoff in Utility-Based Agent & Complete Workflow\nDominant Visual: Escalation path from agent to human, signifying responsible design.\nVisual Choreography: The 'Agent Workflow' diagram persists. From the 'Reason' or 'Act' stage, a clear, animated 'Handoff' path branches off, leading to a 'Human Engineer' icon (e.g., a person with a hard hat). A 'Confidence Gauge' (e.g., a dial) appears near the agent, showing 'Confidence: Low' when the handoff path is activated. The path glows green, labeled 'Responsible Handoff'. The Solara Site anomaly example from earlier briefly reappears, showing the agent routing the anomaly to the human engineer icon.\nPersistence: Clean anchor across steps."
        }
    ],
    "9": [
        {
            "presentation_step_number": "1",
            "title": "Key Takeaways",
            "narration_statement": "Let's bring it together. Every agent is built from four components. Perception, memory, reasoning, and action working together in a continuous loop. That loop drives agent environment interaction. The agent observes, decides, acts, and observes again. The three types of agents, reactive, goal-based, and utility-based represent increasing levels of sophistication. Reactive agents are fast and rule-driven. Goal-based agents plan towards outcomes. Utility-based agents optimize across trade-offs.",
            "matching_section": "Purpose: Explain Key Takeaways in Summary & What's Next\nDominant Visual: Consolidated summary of core components, interaction loop, and agent types.\nVisual Choreography: A central, dynamic 'Summary' panel appears. Three distinct sections within it animate in sequentially: 1. 'Components:' with four small icons (Perception, Memory, Reasoning, Action) arranged in a mini-loop. 2. 'Interaction:' with a simplified circular arrow icon labeled 'Observe -> Decide -> Act'. 3. 'Agent Types:' with three distinct icons (Reactive: gear, Goal-Based: target, Utility-Based: balance scale) with concise tags: [Rule-Driven], [Outcome-Focused], [Optimized]. Each section illuminates as narrated.\nPersistence: Clean anchor across steps."
        },
        {
            "presentation_step_number": "2",
            "title": "What's Next: Hands-On Demo",
            "narration_statement": "Coming up next, we're going hands-on. In the demo, we'll visualize types and architectures of AI, agents, and action as good so you can see the decision loops and execution patterns we've been discussing actually running in real time. Let's take a look.",
